A||||13||Gold-86SE||V6 overhaul||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||01-22-2001||02:35 PM||||<BR>I want your ideas on what I should repair/replace on my 2.8.  I am not doing a swap, and I am not doing a rebuild, more of a reburbish (if that is a possiblity).<P>It appears to have a pretty good oil leak coming from the back valve cover and I plan on replacing the following things in addtion to the valve cover gaskets.  Vacuum hoses, freeze plugs, oil pan gasket, and any thing else that appears to be deteriorating.<P>Additionally, what do you think of pulling the tranny from the block to examine the condition of the clutch?<P>Thanks for the input.<BR><P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||01-22-2001||02:48 PM||||if you have the motor out.. spend the $100 for the new clutch.. you'll kick your self if you install the new motor and wipe out the old tired clutch. is the motor puking or is the gasket just bad? a $10 gasket is way cheaper than a $900 rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||01-22-2001||03:17 PM||||I'd say check the oil pressure before you decide what to do.  If you're still getting 15-20 psi at idle and 50-55 at 2000rpm, then you should be good to go.  If it's too low then I'd want to do a rebuild..||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gold-86SE||01-23-2001||09:35 AM||||<BR>1FST, the motor is not puking, it runs fine.  I just have cracked exhaust manifolds and oil leaks and other heavy maintenance that is required. Right now the engine is still mated to the tranny, hence my question about pulling it apart.  Right now my budget is really strained and probably could not easily afford to pay for a clutch, additionally I don't recall any mechanical problems with my clutch prior to my engine pull this last weekend.<P>Standard, before I pulled the engine the oil pressure was fine, never had any problems. But, I was thinking that I might replace the pump anyway once I pull the oil pan. What do you guys think?<P>Thanks for the answers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||fieroshop||01-23-2001||10:59 AM||||If your oil pressure is good and there is no leak from the oil pan, I would leave it alone. Remember the saying, if it is not broken do not mess with it.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||01-23-2001||11:53 AM||||<BR>Fieroshop, sad fact is that the oil pan gasket is leaking, so I am going to have to pull the pan anyway.<P>Still, should I leave the oil pump alone or replace it while doing this work?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||01-23-2001||01:36 PM||||If the oil pressure is still good I'd leave the pump alone.  My theory is that if put in a new pump the higher oil pressure might cause other gaskets and internal seals to start puking, which wouldn't be fun.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gold-86SE||01-23-2001||02:12 PM||||<BR>Ok, I'm sold, I will not replace the oil pump.  Now, considering the rest of my engine what would you do during this refurb.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Indiana_resto_guy||01-24-2001||12:11 AM||||Replace the timing componets. With the other stuff you've mentioned, you should be fine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||01-24-2001||12:25 AM||||Very good idea, completly forgot about that.  Defenitly put on a new timing chain and gears.  Don't know how much work you want to do, but I'd pull the heads to check the lifters and put on new head gaskets.. check the exhaust manifolds for cracks and replace the gaskets, if you use a gasket for the 3.4 it'll only cost like $20..  new intake manifold gaskets.. new o-rings on the injectors.. I'll post more if I can think of anything else  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||01-24-2001||12:32 AM||||Standard,<P><BR>You got it backards.......the stock Fiero exhaust manifold gaskets are about $20.  The Camaro/Firebird 3.4 gaskets are about $6.<P>I bought mine from AutoZone......part #90864 (Fel-Pro).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Gold-86SE||01-24-2001||09:57 AM||||<BR>So, I am understanding that the exhaust gaskets for a 3.4 fit on the 2.8 just fine and save me some money in the process - saving money is always a good thing.<P>I have never pulled a head off an engine, what type of work am I looking at?  Will I need to replace the bolts, like on the Duke?<P>About timing chain and gears, is there anything special I need to do this work?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||01-24-2001||11:22 AM||||Oops, I thought the 2.8 ones were more along the lines of $40..<P>You might want to skip pulling the heads if you're not having problems with the head gasket or lifters, and save the work for a future rebuild.  You'd have to pull the intake manifold and valve covers, and you'd also have to adjust the valves after you got it back on.  I believe it's always recommended that you replace the bolts, cheap insurance.<P>As for the timing chain, you need a two or three jawed puller to pull of the crankshaft sprocket, but that's the only special tool you need.  <P>If the timing chain was really lose, you'll also want to replace the timing chain damper.  It's only held on by two bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Steven||01-24-2001||11:39 AM||||Yes.Change the timing chain dampner if you put a new chain on the engine. I made the mistake of not doing that, and about a year later my car my car was acting very strange. It would idle very high, cranck very hard and throw out codes all day long.I played with the timing, bought a new distributor, replaced sensors. Nothing worked. Did a compression test on cylinder 1,3,and 5. They all had 35-45 psi. Thought that the rings were shot. Started tearing down the engine, only to find that the timing chain tried to walk off the gears, and had jumped a tooth or two. Now the chain I bought was a Clevite. Major name in auto parts, so I am not blaming them. I think the problem was the cheap guy holding the wallet who did not want to spend a few extra dollars on that part. So buy one.<BR>  ||||reg||1
